# Factory-tree /etc/shadow with NO root line, on purpose.
#
# One generic image installs on every host, so root's password must be a
# per-host value, not a baked one. systemd-firstboot fills it in on first
# boot from the passwd.hashed-password.root credential:
#
#   /usr/lib/credstore/passwd.hashed-password.root          image default
#   $ESP/loader/credentials/passwd.hashed-password.root.cred  per-host
#
# Verified in a VM: firstboot writes the credential's hash only when the
# root entry is ABSENT. It does not overwrite an existing one — not even a
# locked "!*" placeholder — so shipping any root line here, locked or not,
# makes the credential unreachable and pins every host to one password.
#
# The result lands on the /etc overlay upper, so a later `passwd root`
# persists and firstboot never runs again (ConditionFirstBoot=yes).
#
# NINE colon-separated fields when a line is present. The third (lastchg)
# must be EMPTY: an 8-field line shifts min/max/warn left, lastchg=0 means
# "must change at next login", min=99999 then refuses that change, and
# login becomes impossible. That shipped for one release.
#
# Interactive users are declarative too — see credentials/README.md for
# sysusers.extra (classic) and home.create.<name> (homed).
